    "Best practices for the use of bypass conduits in CABG include various strategies such as using radial artery grafts to target vessels with subocclusive stenoses, avoiding the use of the radial artery after transradial catheterization, and using bilateral or surgical artery procedures in patients with coronary artery disease. The internal mammary artery should be harvested using the skeletonization technique to reduce the risk of wound complications, and an endoscopic saphenous vein harvest is recommended in patients at risk of wound complications. The decision to add CABG to patients with significant CAD involves considering various factors such as comorbidities and technical feasibility.": {
      "CASS (Coronary Artery Surgery Study) registry indicates improved survival with LIMA-LAD compared to SVG graft. LIMA grafting reduces death and revascularization. A single RCT showed improved event-free survival with LIMA arm at 10 years. BIMA's benefit is supported by observational studies and a meta-analysis of 38 studies. A noted decreased mortality rate with BIMA use. However, a high rate of crossover was noted from BIMA to single IMA. Increasing BIMA volume is associated with better survival outcomes.": {
        "Recommendations for CABG in patients undergoing cardiac surgery include its use in patients with significant CAD for reducing ischemic events, and it may be reasonable for those with intermediate CAD.": {
          "Additional considerations include the risk of sternal infection with BIMA grafting and the importance of surgical expertise.": {
            "A multidisciplinary discussion can help weigh risks and benefits of CABG, especially for patients with jeopardized myocardium and reduced ejection fraction.": {
              "Various studies support the use of BIMA grafting in CABG and highlight the associated mortality benefits and potential complications.": {}
